\begin{align*}
\frac{2v_1 + v_2}{5}\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} &= \begin{pmatrix} \frac{38}{5} \\ \frac{19}{5} \end{pmatrix} \\
\frac{2v_1 + 3v_2}{13}\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} &= \begin{pmatrix} \frac{58}{13} \\ \frac{87}{13} \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}
\]
Simplifying these equations, we get:
\[
\begin{align*}
2v_1 + v_2 &= 19 \\
2v_1 + 3v_2 &= 29
\end{align*}
\]
Subtracting the first equation from the second gives us $2v_2 = 10$, so $v_2 = 5$. Substituting $v_2 = 5$ into the first equation, we get $2v_1 + 5 = 19$, which simplifies to $2v_1 = 14$, so $v_1 = 7$. Therefore, $\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 7 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}$.</think> <answer> $\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}}$</answer>

